{
  "requirement": "An application-supplied assessment procedure can perform ordinary computation\nand request zero or more typed model answers. It can use intermediate answers\nin later computation and requests, then return its `Criterion::Ok` value.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The tests cover each obligation in `requirements.sdk.graph.agent-calls.procedures`. I read the test `Memory` fixture and its `impl[Review/Criterion<Memory>]`. I did not read the `MockAgent` source.\n\n- **Ordinary computation and zero model answers:** `runner_reuses_negative_judgments_and_records_only_callback_observations` runs the `Review` procedure with `ask_in_callback` false and no agent configured. The procedure makes no model requests and still returns its `Criterion::Ok` value. The test asserts that the result is `!j.result` and that the procedure ran once.\n- **One or more typed model answers:** `runner_does_not_store_a_missing_agent_error_and_supports_intermediate_calls` sets `ask_in_callback: true`. `impl[Memory]/fn[run_review]` then makes two `ctx.ask::<Answer>()` requests, each deserialized into the typed `Answer { n: usize }`. The test asserts exactly 2 agent requests. `shared_context_allows_independent_concurrent_agent_calls` also makes typed requests and asserts `first.n == 7` and `second.n == 11`.\n- **Intermediate answers used in later computation and requests:** In `run_review`, the second ask's context is built with `format!(\"Check intermediate result {}\", first.n)`. The test asserts `requests[1].0 == \"Check intermediate result 7\"`, so the first answer reached the second request. The procedure also asserts `second.n == first.n`, which is computation on both answers.\n- **Returning the `Criterion::Ok` value:** The procedure returns a `Judgment` with `result: node != 9`. The tests assert both outcomes: `.result` is true for node 1 and false for node 9. The `check` calls confirm the stored judgment.\n\nI found no gaps. The remaining obligations, such as caching, dependency recording and missing-agent errors, belong to other requirements that the same tests also cite.",
